# barqsharingd — the unprivileged half of Barq.
#
# No capabilities at all. `inet` is a supplementary GROUP, not a capability: it is
# what lets the process CREATE a socket, and nothing more.
#
# Creating one is not the same as being allowed to send on it. Since Android B,
# reaching the LOCAL network additionally needs a per-uid bit in a BPF map that only
# uid 0 and uid 1000 get for free -- see config/barq_aid.txt. Without it this daemon
# starts, advertises, and every mDNS send fails with EPERM. `inet` alone is enough for
# AirDrop, which rides mosey0 and is not gated; it is not enough for Quick Share.
#
# Started after barqd, though it does not require it: with no link up it simply
# reports linkUp=false rather than failing.

# Received archives land here. Created by init rather than by the daemon so the
# ownership and mode are set by something that has the standing to set them, and so
# the directory exists before the first transfer rather than on the first write.
#
# 0700 system_ext_barq: only this daemon can read what a peer sent until the app collects it.
# It was 0700 nobody, which is weaker than it looks -- `nobody` is shared, so every
# other process running as it could read a received file before the app collected it.
# The ownership MUST move with the uid: miss it and the daemon boots cleanly and then
# cannot write its own inbox, which fails at the first transfer rather than at boot.
on post-fs-data
    mkdir /data/misc/barq 0700 system_ext_barq system_ext_barq
    mkdir /data/misc/barq/inbox 0700 system_ext_barq system_ext_barq

service barqsharingd /system_ext/bin/barqsharingd
    class late_start
    user system_ext_barq
    group system_ext_barq inet
    disabled
    oneshot false
    restart_period 10

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    start barqsharingd
